`]}),e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsxs(t.th,{children:["Parameter (",e.jsx(a,{math:"m",display:!1})," = target width ÷ proxy width)"]}),e.jsxs(t.th,{children:["μP rule under Adam (",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\sigma^2",display:!1,trail:","})," ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\eta",display:!1,trail:":"})," the proxy's values)"]})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Hidden matrices"}),e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Init variance ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\sigma^2/m",display:!1,trail:","})," learning rate ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\eta/m",display:!1})]})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Embeddings, norm gains, biases"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Init and learning rate unchanged"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Output logits"}),e.jsxs(t.td,{children:["Multiplied by ",e.jsx(a,{math:"1/m",display:!1})]})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Attention logits"}),e.jsxs(t.td,{children:[e.jsx(a,{math:"1/d_{\\mathrm{head}}",display:!1})," instead of ",e.jsx(a,{math:"1/\\sqrt{d_{\\mathrm{head}}}",display:!1})]})]})]})]}),e.jsxs("div",{"data-layout":"mup-attention-scaling-comparison",className:"grid min-w-0 grid-cols-1 gap-3 md:grid-cols-2",children:[e.jsx(a,{math:"\\Theta\\!\\left(1 / \\sqrt{d_{\\mathrm{head}}}\\right)",layout:"stacked",label:"Standard attention"}),e.jsx(a,{math:"\\Theta\\!\\left(1 / d_{\\mathrm{head}}\\right)",layout:"stacked",label:"Common μP rule"})]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Each update's effect stays constant across width",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["μP gives each kind of layer a rule in the width ratio ",e.jsx(a,{math:"m",display:!1,trail:","})," so a proxy tuned under the same rules hands its best settings to the target without a new search ",e.jsx(s,{id:"yang2022mup",children:"Yang et al., 2021"}),". The table is the form for Adam in Yang et al.'s Tensor Programs V. Other, equivalent forms exist, so no single row, such as the attention scale, defines μP on its own."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Why ",e.jsx(a,{math:"1/m",display:!1})," for a hidden matrix? Take an input ",e.jsx(a,{math:"x",display:!1})," of width ",e.jsx(a,{math:"n",display:!1,trail:","})," so each output coordinate sums ",e.jsx(a,{math:"n",display:!1})," products. Adam's update ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\Delta W",display:!1})," has entries about the size of the learning rate ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\eta",display:!1,trail:"."})," The update is correlated with the layer's input, so the products in ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\Delta W \\, x",display:!1})," add up instead of cancelling, and the output moves by about ",e.jsx(a,{math:"n\\eta",display:!1,trail:"."})," A learning rate proportional to ",e.jsx(a,{math:"1/n",display:!1,trail:","})," and so to ",e.jsx(a,{math:"1/m",display:!1,trail:","})," keeps that change constant at any width. Tensor Programs V's Appendix J gives this argument and calls its Adam version an intuition not yet formalized ",e.jsx(s,{id:"yang2022mup",children:"Yang et al., 2021"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Tensor Programs V derives the other rows from the same count ",e.jsx(s,{id:"yang2022mup",children:"Yang et al., 2021"}),". An output logit sums ",e.jsx(a,{math:"n",display:!1})," products of output weights with a hidden vector that is correlated with them, so it is multiplied by ",e.jsx(a,{math:"1/m",display:!1,trail:"."})," An attention logit ",e.jsx(a,{math:"q \\cdot k",display:!1})," sums ",e.jsx(a,{math:"d_{\\mathrm{head}}",display:!1})," products. At initialization ",e.jsx(a,{math:"q",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(a,{math:"k",display:!1})," are uncorrelated, so the sum grows as ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\sqrt{d_{\\mathrm{head}}}",display:!1,trail:","})," which the standard ",e.jsx(a,{math:"1/\\sqrt{d_{\\mathrm{head}}}",display:!1})," offsets. During training they become correlated, the sum grows as ",e.jsx(a,{math:"d_{\\mathrm{head}}",display:!1,trail:","})," and the common μP rule divides by ",e.jsx(a,{math:"d_{\\mathrm{head}}",display:!1,trail:"."})," The slide's ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\Theta",display:!1}),' means "grows like, up to a constant factor". Embeddings, gains and biases sum over no dimension that grows with width, so their rules stay unchanged.']}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The paper reports two separate transfer pairs: 13M parameters to BERT-large at 350M, and about 40M to GPT-3 6.7B ",e.jsx(s,{id:"yang2022mup",children:"Yang et al., 2021"}),'. The optional slide "μP in released models" names released models that ship width transfer.']}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Lingle tested μP up to 10B parameters, with proxies about 4,000 times smaller. In most settings the learning rate transferred approximately, with some drift. It did not transfer with trainable RMSNorm gains, or with AdamW's usual weight decay, which this literature calls coupled. Transfer also failed with the standard ",e.jsx(a,{math:"1/\\sqrt{d_{\\mathrm{head}}}",display:!1})," attention scale and with Lion, an optimizer whose update is ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\pm 1",display:!1})," in every coordinate. The attention-scale failure vanished in float32, and the Lion failure vanished over several seeds ",e.jsx(s,{id:"lingle2024mutransfer",children:"Lingle, 2024"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Week 5 and the μP literature use opposite names for this weight decay. Week 5 calls it decoupled because the decay term bypasses Adam's division by the second-moment estimate. The μP literature calls it coupled because the decay is multiplied by the learning rate: each step shrinks a weight by the fraction ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\eta\\lambda",display:!1,trail:"."})," So μP's ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\eta/m",display:!1})," weakens the decay as the model widens. Multiplying ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\lambda",display:!1})," by ",e.jsx(a,{math:"m",display:!1})," keeps ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\eta\\lambda",display:!1})," fixed and restores transfer ",e.jsx(s,{id:"kosson2025weightdecay",children:"Kosson et al., 2026"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Released Muon models follow two conventions. ",e.jsx(s,{id:"arcee2026trinitylarge",paren:!1,children:"Arcee Trinity"})," and ",e.jsx(s,{id:"karpathy2026nanochat",paren:!1,children:"nanochat"})," scale each update by ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\sqrt{\\max(1,\\, \\text{fan\\_out}/\\text{fan\\_in})}",display:!1,trail:","})," where ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"fan_in"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"fan_out"})," are the matrix's input and output widths. Arcee reports that this convention transfers the learning rate across width. ",e.jsx(s,{id:"kimiteam2025kimik2",paren:!1,children:"Kimi K2"}),", ",e.jsx(s,{id:"glmteam2025glm45",paren:!1,children:"GLM-4.5"}),", ",e.jsx(s,{id:"deepseekai2026deepseekv4",paren:!1,children:"DeepSeek-V4"}),", ",e.jsx(s,{id:"poolside2026laguna",paren:!1,children:"Laguna"})," and ",e.jsx(s,{id:"qwen2026qwen38flashnext",paren:!1,children:"Qwen3.8-Flash-Next"})," use Week 5's Moonlight rescale. That rescale matches the size of Muon's update to AdamW's ",e.jsx(s,{id:"liu2025moonlight",children:"Liu et al., 2025"}),". It sets no rule for how the learning rate changes with width. Laguna and Qwen3.8-Flash-Next take their learning rate from fitted laws."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Width rules leave length and batch open",accent:6,number:"7",children:[e.jsx(a,{math:"\\eta^{*} \\propto D^{-\\beta},\\quad \\beta \\approx 0.3 \\text{ to } 0.5 \\quad (\\text{fixed batch})",label:"Learning rate against training tokens"}),e.jsx(a,{math:"B_{\\mathrm{opt}} \\propto D^{0.38},\\qquad \\eta \\propto \\sqrt{B}\\ \\ (B < B_{\\mathrm{crit}})",label:"Batch against the token budget"}),e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
